WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.128 --> 00:00:06.920
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN:
Hi, welcome to session,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.300
serverless functions--

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.100
FaaS-- that are secure,
scalable, resilient anywhere.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.000
My name is Vinod, and I'm a
product manager at Google.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.740
And I am presenting with
my colleague Dustin Ingram,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.380
and Matthew Lawson from IKEA.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.700
So let's get started.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.788
So here's today's agenda.

00:00:25.788 --> 00:00:28.080
First, we'll give you an
overview of the product, Cloud

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.450
Functions.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.440
Then we'll walk you
through key function

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.265
as a service use cases.

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:35.140
Then we'll follow up
with an interesting case

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.720
study from IKEA and their
digital transformation

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.250
with serverless.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.650
After that, we'll walk
you through a key security

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.950
capabilities and diagnosability
and availability capabilities

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.380
in serverless at
Google Cloud, and then

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.720
walk you through our
functions everywhere vision.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.490
So let's get started.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.590
Cloud Functions--
Cloud Functions

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.060
as Google's
event-driven serverless

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.160
compute platform
where users can write

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.200
small snippets of
code that react

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.750
to events as well as HTTP.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.970
You can write up these
functions very easily

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:08.450
and get started to process
small snippets of code,

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.120
say, for example, uploading a
file to Google Cloud Storage

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.370
and do some processing, also
wrap up with some ML APIs,

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.180
do some ML processing
on the file,

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.830
and then store the
results back in the cloud.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.540
Cloud Functions is
truly serverless,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.660
where you pay only
for what you use.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.480
It scales seamlessly with zero
infrastructure management.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.550
It also has a streamlined
development model

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.390
where you can wrap up and
write a function very quickly.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.600
In addition, it's
integrated with key events

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.913
all across GCP to build up
complicated applications

00:01:38.913 --> 00:01:39.455
and services.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.100
When we talk to
users, users really

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.830
like to use Cloud Functions
for its simplicity,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.690
scalability, the
fact that you don't

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.050
have to manage servers, the
pay for use model, and the fact

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.170
that it's connected to a
broad range of cloud services.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.150
Key functions as a
service use cases

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.910
that our customers use
Cloud Functions are things

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.780
like servers web hooks, where
users can write up functions

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.870
with GitHub notifications that
are processed and then served

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.900
in Slack; further,
real-time data processing,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.260
wherein users can upload data
to the cloud, to Cloud Storage,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.250
triggered a function to do some
processing, call an ML API,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.150
and then store the results
in the cloud again;

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.240
serverless IoT backends,
where users have

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.170
IoT systems all
across the world,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.622
sending things like sensor
and temperature data.

00:02:30.622 --> 00:02:32.330
And then that's
processed through Pub/Sub

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.150
messaging systems that
trigger Cloud Functions.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.800
And then you do
some IoT analytics,

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.990
and then store the
results in the cloud.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.390
And then our intelligent
applications, using

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.870
AI in BigQuery, for example,
chat boards using Twilio,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.380
where functions are
wired up to invoke

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.470
an ML AP or a natural language
processing API, as well

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.830
as doing sentiment analysis
with that and trend analysis

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.420
with BigQuery.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.940
Along with these
key FaaS use cases,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.210
when we talk to customers,
they have several key FaaS user

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.180
needs that come up.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.580
And these needs are things
like developer productivity,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.460
building complex enterprise
workflows with serverless

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:14.370
and functions, building
applications that are secure,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.520
and then having the ability
to build applications

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.580
with multiple languages,
with diagnosability,

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.590
debugging capabilities,
telemetry,

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.530
and local development.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.200
Further, users will care
about global availability

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.590
and scalability to ensure the
application and function scale

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.390
for their application traffic.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.150
In addition, users really
care about portability

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.150
and can we reduce vendor lock-in
when we build our serverless

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.370
applications?

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.420
And to meet these user
needs, Cloud Functions

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.850
offers the following
key differentiators.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.810
First is ease of use.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.200
Cloud Functions is a
very easy-to-use product,

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.500
where users can write up
a function very quickly

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.930
from the UI, from the CLI,
from their favorite IDE,

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.040
and from an API.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.065
For example, here's a
simple function in Node.js

00:04:04.065 --> 00:04:06.810
where a user's just written
"Hello, Cloud Next!"

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:11.160
The user quickly writes it up
and deploys it using the CLI.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.770
And then it's ready,
serving traffic.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.980
All in a single command, the
user is writing their code.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.690
It's getting built in the cloud.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.810
It's getting deployed in the
cloud with health checks.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.910
And then it's, within
minutes, running

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.380
and ready to serve traffic.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.470
This is really powerful, that
all in a single command, a user

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.960
can get this and
achieve so much.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.900
Along with the ease of
use, Cloud Functions

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.110
offers the ability to build
complex end-to-end solutions.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.520
These end-to-end
solutions can be

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.280
built using key powerful
building blocks, which

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:45.910
are event-based, for
things like data processing

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.210
with ML APIs, and BigQuery, and
so on, and then for automation

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.660
workloads, with things such as
Cloud Scheduler, Cloud Tasks,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.822
and so on and so forth.

00:04:54.822 --> 00:04:57.280
And to walk you through this
application journey of bidding

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.330
end-to-end applications,
I would like

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.480
to now invite Matthew
Lawson from IKEA to walk you

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.580
through IKEA's
digital transformation

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.447
with serverless and
Cloud Functions.

00:05:06.447 --> 00:05:08.780
MATTHEW LAWSON: Hello, and
good morning, good afternoon,

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.405
or good evening,
depending on what time

00:05:10.405 --> 00:05:12.682
it is wherever
you're watching this.

00:05:12.682 --> 00:05:14.890
I'm very happy to do this
presentation for you today.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:15.970
But of course, I
would have loved

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:17.230
to have done this in person.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.450
But because of the
current global situation,

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.400
I get to do this from my
home office here in Sweden.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.520
So in this talk, I'm going
to explain to you how

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.250
IKEA is using Google Cloud
Functions and other serverless

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.800
technologies in our
engineering efforts

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.340
as we go through a
digital transformation.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.720
So my name is Matthew Lawson.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.790
And I work at IKEA as
an engineering manager

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:38.710
here in southern Sweden.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.630
I usually don't start
my presentations off

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.080
with who IKEA is, but I do have
a couple of company slides--

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.018
two of them, to be exact.

00:05:46.018 --> 00:05:47.560
So first, maybe I
can share something

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.640
that you might not know about
IKEA, and that is our vision.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.383
It has very little
to do with furniture.

00:05:53.383 --> 00:05:55.300
Actually, it has nothing
to do with furniture.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.340
And that is to create
a better everyday life

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.745
for the many people.

00:05:58.745 --> 00:06:00.870
So whether it's becoming
one of the world's leaders

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.000
in sustainability, starting
to sell solar panels,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.860
working with
international charities,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:09.070
or creating that great furniture
for the home, all of this

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.260
to help the many people
to create that better

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.618
everyday life.

00:06:12.618 --> 00:06:15.160
But the two words out of that
vision that mean the most to me

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:16.848
are "many people."

00:06:16.848 --> 00:06:19.390
And to let you know what I mean,
let me give you some numbers

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.320
here.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.440
So IKEA is over 76 years
old, and is made up

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.670
of many companies,
organizations,

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.835
and hundreds of thousands of
co-workers across the globe.

00:06:28.835 --> 00:06:31.210
So here are some highlights
from our previous fiscal year

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.460
as of September 1, 2019.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:37.120
So IKEA had 433 stores
open, with 12 of them

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.680
opening that fiscal year.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.470
And actually, within the last
few months of this recording,

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.900
we have over 450 now.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.940
We have 211,000 co-workers
working for us globally.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:50.350
And this is growing, especially
in our digital organizations

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:52.310
across the globe.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.130
And we're now opening small
stores in city centers.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.260
As an example of this,
within the first four months

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.540
of opening, we had
a million visitors

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:03.140
to our IKEA La Madeleine
store in downtown Paris.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:05.630
We have billions of visitors
to our website each year.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:09.230
And we have over 100 million
IKEA family, or our loyalty

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:12.120
program, across
the globe as well.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:15.110
So this means that when we
create a new physical product

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:18.680
or a new digital product, like
a mobile web, web application,

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:20.450
or an API, we need
to make sure we

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:25.380
cater to those many people,
the millions of people.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.600
So IKEA, as in many
different companies,

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.910
are going through a
digital transformation.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.260
IKEA has been around since 1958.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.850
And to be completely
transparent,

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:37.170
we have quite a bit of technical
debt across the organization,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.450
or what we like to call
it is our spaghetti

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.250
in the data center.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.960
Our digital transformation
was not simply

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.820
a matter of migrating
to the cloud.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.530
One decision we
made early on was

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.318
how this was going to happen.

00:07:48.318 --> 00:07:49.860
We did not want to
do a complete lift

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.170
and shift to a cloud partner.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.310
But instead, we
decided to re-engineer

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:57.000
the organization and digital
products at the same time.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.740
Our chief operations
manager stated

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.780
once, "We are flying
a plane and changing

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.637
the engine at the same time."

00:08:02.637 --> 00:08:04.470
Personally, to me, this
is quite hard to do.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:07.320
But as an engineer, it
has been a ton of fun.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.330
So our new solutions
and projects

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.555
are striving to be
completely cloud-native.

00:08:11.555 --> 00:08:12.930
And this is quite
hard to do when

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.420
the brain of your
application, the data,

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.280
is still sitting on premise.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.530
And we're doing a lot of
work in that area internally

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.170
as well, creating new data
pipelines to cater both

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.820
to the old and the new.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:27.540
So one of the use cases that
we started re-engineering

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:28.980
that is heavily
using Google Cloud

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.070
Functions and many other
serverless technologies

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.440
is our almost live feed
of our inventory system

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.593
that we have at each IKEA
location across the globe.

00:08:37.593 --> 00:08:39.510
So this enables us to
show a live feed of what

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.360
is selling, allowing us to
export that data to BigQuery

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:46.192
for further analysis, and also
to create dashboards to give

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:48.150
the store insights on
what is happening instead

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.070
of that daily or weekly report.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:52.810
And I'm sure there is
much more use cases

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.725
that we haven't even
thought up of yet here.

00:08:55.725 --> 00:08:57.600
So let's take a look at
a architecture of one

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.930
of the use cases that my fellow
co-worker Michael created.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.430
First, we have our
inventory server,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:06.240
which sits on premise
at each IKEA location

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.130
and talks to the Google Cloud
through a Compute Engine

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:13.770
from the shared VPC on a VPN.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.810
And this sends many
different messages as XML.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.830
The XML is then compared to
Google Cloud Storage, looking

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.380
for new or changed rows.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.870
And the ones that are changed
are sent, as JSON object,

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.862
to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:09:27.862 --> 00:09:29.320
We then have a
batch receiving side

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:32.620
of this, which uses Google Cloud
Functions and actually powers

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.720
this entire architecture.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.580
This pulls in
messages from Pub/Sub

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:39.320
and puts the raw data into
many different BigQuery data

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.415
sets depending on
the message type.

00:09:41.415 --> 00:09:42.790
And we can also
move that message

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.630
on for further processing
using other Pub/Sub topics.

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:48.995
Finally, we're pulling
in the messages

00:09:48.995 --> 00:09:51.370
from the capturing Google
Cloud function using a schedule

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.280
system as well, that
was built. And this

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:57.160
is using Cloud Scheduler,
another Google Cloud function,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.320
Cloud Tasks, and
this configuration

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:01.540
data stored in Firestore.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.850
So this tells the Google Cloud
function, the capturing one,

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.450
how often to execute
depending on some criteria

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:11.170
like the amount of messages
in the queue, time,

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:14.260
and also optimizes everything
from a cost perspective

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:15.680
as well.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:17.680
We then can create
custom dashboards

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.420
using the BigQuery data sets.

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.130
And we can also
process it further on,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:24.010
like I said, with
Pub/Sub topics.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.730
One aspect of this architecture
that we're currently working on

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:30.030
is the ingestion of data
from the on-premise server

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.168
to the cloud.

00:10:31.168 --> 00:10:33.210
We're looking at how we
can do this without using

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:36.170
a VPN tunnel or a shared VPC.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.700
So some people
have asked me, why

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.380
are we using Google
Cloud Functions here

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.240
and not something like Dataflow.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.190
Well, one thing we like
to say here at IKEA

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.950
is we do not respect a solution
until we know what it costs.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.628
And if the solution was to be
turned into mission-critical,

00:10:49.628 --> 00:10:51.170
then we could change
the architecture

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.810
to better support message
ordering, duplication,

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.760
and other aspects.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:58.790
This solution currently
handles between 60 and--

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.970
40,000 to 60,000 messages
per minute and can

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.730
certainly scale
higher, as we've seen.

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.670
And it actually costs
around $30 US per month

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:11.600
to operate to get the data
from on premise to Google Cloud

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.310
Functions.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.220
And so this really showcases
the power of serverless

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:19.700
without needing to
spend a ton of money.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.200
So let's take a
quick look at a demo

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.960
that my colleagues Axel
and Joel created to show

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.400
what you can do with this data.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.300
So here is a map of North
America with our stores.

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.240
And for fun, we're just
showing our inventory changes

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:36.590
from our iconic product
the FRAKTA blue bag.

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:39.170
So as you can see here,
we're getting some messages

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.590
popping in from sales that
are happening in those stores.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.450
And this really
showcases how you

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:46.280
can use serverless
technology to bridge

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.100
between what you're doing
on premise to new cloud

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:49.880
technologies.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:55.430
So with that, if you have
any other further questions,

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:57.080
you can please feel
free to contact me.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:58.640
My email address is here.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.700
And also, as I mentioned,
we're also hiring.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.730
So please visit join.ingka.com
for more information

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.870
around that.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.910
And with that, I thank
you for listening.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:08.830
And please stay safe out there.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:09.640
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN:
Thank you, Matthew,

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.280
for that amazing presentation
on IKEA's digital transformation

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.740
with serverless and
Cloud Functions.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:16.960
Now moving on, one of
the key user needs,

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.610
as well as security--

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:22.210
Cloud Functions offers key
security differentiators

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:24.460
for users in serverless.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.430
One of the key features we offer
is per-function identities,

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:30.580
where we provide fine-grained
control on which users

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.953
are privileged to
invoke a function.

00:12:32.953 --> 00:12:34.870
And we take it a step
further and also provide

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:39.110
around which specific resources
a function has access to.

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:42.040
For example, here's a user
who's trying to invoke a Cloud

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:44.410
Function using Cloud Scheduler.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.180
And you know that
user is authorized.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.070
And the user can
invoke the function.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.570
And then there's
a malicious user

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.340
who is trying to invoke
the same function

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:52.810
and is prevented to do so.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:54.880
Likewise, here is
a Cloud Function

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:58.180
that has permissions
to access BigQuery,

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.120
but it does not have permissions
to access Cloud Storage.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.930
By providing these fine-grained
controls on security,

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:08.470
we really help users control
who can access the function

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.870
and which resources a
function can access, and then

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.470
take the security
level a step further.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:18.130
Along with that, users
really care about VPC.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:21.780
They have systems across the
cloud as well as on prem,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:23.440
in VPC networks.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.900
And our Cloud Functions
play really nice

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:27.280
with these VPC networks.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.920
Functions can connect
with VPC networks.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.590
And we take it a step
further by providing

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:36.010
what is called VPC SC,
or a security perimeter,

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.300
to prevent things like
data exfiltration.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:42.450
Along with fine-grained
access and VPC,

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.250
users are really
concerned about the denial

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:45.990
of wallet attack in serverless.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:49.170
For instance, if a
user service gets

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.880
a lot of requests
from a malicious user,

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.380
they could, at the
end of the month,

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:56.640
get a shocking bill if
their scaling is not capped.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:59.010
To prevent this, we
offer a service called

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:03.990
max instances that let users
cap their serverless services,

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:07.600
thereby preventing the
denial of wallet attack,

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.600
and also letting their
serverless services scale

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.500
to the extent as needed and
within their monthly budgets.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.630
Along with scalability
and security,

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:19.530
users really care about multiple
languages, diagnosability,

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.450
and local development.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.180
Cloud Functions, today, is
available in key languages

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.990
such as Python, Node, and Go.

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:29.650
Further, we're super
excited to announce Java 11,

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:34.480
which is generally available,
as well as .NET and Ruby and we

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:37.240
have more runtimes
coming soon as well.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.810
And to talk to you more about
diagnosability and fault

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.940
tolerance, I would like to
know invite Dustin on stage

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.280
to give you a demo
in this space.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.980
DUSTIN INGRAM: Hi I'm Dustin
Ingram, developer advocate

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:49.510
for Google Cloud.

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:52.540
Let's take a look at how we can
build a reliable, resilient,

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:57.450
self-healing system using Cloud
Functions and Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:00.480
Let's say we've got a fleet
of deployed IoT sensors

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.330
that are sending temperature
data in degrees Fahrenheit,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:07.740
along with a sensor ID and
JSON payload, via an HTTP post,

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.540
to a Cloud Function.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.850
Now say we want to take
that temperature data

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:15.270
and write it to Firestore, which
is our operational database,

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:18.240
and also write it to BigQuery,
which is our data analytics

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:19.680
warehouse.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:21.300
Traditionally, we
would probably do

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.930
this in the same application,
or in the same function.

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:27.160
But this has a couple drawbacks.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.610
So if, for example, the
code that writes to BigQuery

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:32.340
is not available,
or has a bug in it--

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:34.230
or maybe there's a
networking glitch,

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:35.940
and it can't talk
to the service.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.130
Now the function errors out.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.900
And you're left
in a weird state.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:41.910
Should I rerun the function?

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:44.530
But then, what if Firestore
write did succeed,

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:46.860
and now I've written
to Firestore twice?

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:50.500
What is the overall
state of my application?

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:51.960
So we want to decouple these.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.210
Now one way we could
do this is to put it

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.690
into two Cloud Functions,
one that writes to Firestore

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.700
and one that writes to BigQuery.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.230
And this is a step in
the right direction.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:05.250
But that postTemperature()
function is still responsible

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.430
for running, and retrying, and
understanding if those other

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:11.200
functions succeeded or failed.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.260
So we want to decouple
these completely.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.230
And to do that, we'll
use Cloud Pub/Sub, which

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:19.980
is a high-scale messaging
bus on Google Cloud.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:23.490
Now all postTemperature()
has to do is write a message

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:26.370
to the Pub/Sub bus,
and then it's done.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.920
The writeBigQuery() and
writeFirestore() functions are

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:33.870
listening for messages coming in
on that topic in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:37.080
When they get those messages,
they'll do their functionality.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.240
And they'll fail and
retry independently

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.610
from each other asynchronously.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.950
Let's take a look
at this in action.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:47.100
Here are the three Cloud
functions that we talked about,

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.380
postTemperature(),
writeBigQuery(),

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.060
and writeFirestore().

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:54.390
I've written a shell script to
simulate the IoT sensor posting

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.820
their data via an HTTP post.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:59.610
So they're going to post it
to this sensors collection

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:00.750
in Firestore.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:03.030
And you can see these
values coming in.

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:04.770
But we've also got
a tracking sheet

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:07.500
that we wrote that this actually
looks at the timestamps coming

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:09.869
in and puts in a message
when it's about to try

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:10.829
writing to BigQuery--

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:13.050
I'm about to try
writing to Firestore--

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:16.030
and then whether it
succeeded or failed.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:18.990
And you can see these
update in real time.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:21.300
Everything is good, so
it's all successful.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:24.030
But I want to simulate an
error in the, let's say,

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:26.020
writeBigQuery() function.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.480
So I'm going to open up the
editor here in Cloud Functions.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.290
There's a built-in
editor, but you can also

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:34.050
use your normal IDE,
your normal editor,

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.550
to deploy from your
workstation and so on.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.420
But this is handy for
doing quick edits.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.180
So I'm going to go here, and
I'm going to intentionally break

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.120
this function I'm going to
change the name of the table

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.050
from readings to, let's just
say, readings with a few S's.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:52.440
So maybe one of our Python
developers made a typo

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.090
and put the wrong
name of the table.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.500
So let's deploy this.

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:59.310
So I'm deploying a broken
version of the writeBigQuery()

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:00.252
function.

00:18:00.252 --> 00:18:01.710
And while that's
deploying, you can

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:03.752
look at some of the other
attributes of our Cloud

00:18:03.752 --> 00:18:04.680
function.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:06.900
You can set identity so
your function can maybe

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:08.910
only access certain resources.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:11.140
Or you can set
environment variables--

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:14.810
for example, the sheet ID
that we're doing updating in

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.650
All right, so now that
our function is deployed,

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:20.850
I'm going to rerun that shell
script that simulates 10

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.340
IoT sensor readings coming in.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.770
And let's go look at
it in Google Sheets.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:28.260
So you can see it's
succeeding and trying.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:30.720
And you can see it's
happening asynchronously.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.370
Those functions are running
and failing independently.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.610
We've set Cloud
Functions to retry,

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:37.330
which means that if
it gets an error,

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.080
it will retry immediately.

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:40.950
If it gets another
error again, it'll

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.680
wait a bit longer, and
so on and so forth,

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.800
as an exponential back-off.

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.980
All right, so those
are all failed.

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:51.650
So we want to figure
out, OK, what went wrong?

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:53.500
Now I know what went
wrong, because I

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:54.578
put that typo in there.

00:18:54.578 --> 00:18:56.620
But let's pretend that we
didn't, and we actually

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.070
need to debug this.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.160
Cloud Logging
automatically picks up all

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:03.700
the logging messages from Cloud
Functions, but also Cloud Run,

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:06.920
App Engine, Kubernetes,
and a bunch of others.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:09.160
And you can see, we can
identify our error here.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:12.880
The table "cloud funcs
readingsssss" is not available.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.450
So Cloud Logging is great.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:16.300
You can do monitoring
and alerting.

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:18.310
You can have it
automatically aggregate error

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:20.750
reports using error reporting.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.690
And it's a really
handy and useful tool

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:26.050
for debugging your serverless
code on Google Cloud.

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:28.390
So let's fix our
BigQuery function

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.250
I'm going to load
the editor back up

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:33.580
and fix it so that instead of
"readingsssss" with many S's,

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:38.740
it's just "readings" with one S.
And then we will redeploy this.

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:40.360
Now let's check
our tracking sheet,

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.810
where you can see it's actually
been retrying this whole time.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:46.970
As it retries again with the
new version of writeBigQuery()

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:49.780
it'll start to succeed
now instead of failing.

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:52.840
And again, all we did was
redeploy the function.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:54.980
We had to set Cloud
Functions to retry

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:56.382
so the data wouldn't be lost.

00:19:56.382 --> 00:19:58.090
Because the system is
keeping track of it

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.880
internally as it retries
the function with the same

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:04.370
payload again and again
until it succeeds.

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:06.220
And that'll happen
for up to seven days,

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.440
retrying your Cloud
function so you don't have

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.490
to worry about losing data.

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:14.610
And that is how you can
build a reliable, resilient,

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.597
self-healing system
with Cloud Functions.

00:20:16.597 --> 00:20:18.180
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN:
Thank you, Dustin,

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:20.430
for that amazing demo on
diagnosability and fault

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:22.320
tolerance.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.500
Now moving on, when users are
using serverless and Cloud

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:29.070
Functions, they really care
about local development

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:30.540
and disconnected development.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.430
Imagine that you're
working a team,

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:36.330
and you have take a flight,
a seven-hour flight,

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:38.760
and you want to continue
developing your application

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:40.620
that you're working
with on your team.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:42.938
You can now do that
with Cloud Functions

00:20:42.938 --> 00:20:43.980
as well as on your plane.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:46.800
And then when you land, you
can connect with your team

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.840
and share your work as well.

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.870
To do this, let's unpack
what's really in your function.

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:56.920
A function is running
an operating system

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:59.670
for a particular
runtime such as Node.js

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.320
with a web framework for
that particular runtime,

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:05.530
such as Express, with
the Functions Framework,

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:08.800
and then with the user's
code and dependencies.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:12.130
Everything in green below
is managed by Google.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:15.633
And only the users code is
what is provided by the user.

00:21:15.633 --> 00:21:17.050
And what we have
done at Google is

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:18.758
we have taken the
second green box, which

00:21:18.758 --> 00:21:21.610
is the function's framework
for each language and runtime,

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:23.320
and open-sourced it.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:26.720
We call this the
Functions Framework.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:28.670
For example, here is
the Functions Framework

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.190
for Node.js on Cloud Functions.

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:32.750
Now with this
Functions Framework,

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:34.940
a user can run a
function locally

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:37.210
with their favorite IDE.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.320
And here's an example
of the Node.js Functions

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.740
Framework, along with a Node
function, in your favorite IDE.

00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:45.200
And then a user can
basically step through code,

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:47.120
write code when
they're on a plane,

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.910
and debug and test it, and
then [INAUDIBLE] with that team

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:51.680
when they land down.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:53.900
Along with
diagnosability, language,

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:55.610
and local development,
users really

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:58.070
care about availability
and scalability

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:00.020
for their serverless services.

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:03.620
Cloud Functions is available
in a broad range of regions.

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:05.450
And today, we are super
excited to announce

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:08.240
that we have added 30 new
regions over the past year

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:12.500
for Cloud Functions, making
the total number of regions 19

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:14.075
where the product is offered.

00:22:14.075 --> 00:22:15.950
With this global
availability of the service,

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:18.380
with super excited to
provide it to our users

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:21.320
in the broad range of
markets in the world.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.080
Along with availability,
users really

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:25.450
care about scalability for
their serverless services

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:26.710
and functions.

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:29.810
Cloud Functions is backed
by Google's infrastructure,

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.380
which runs with [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.840
And it uses a technology
called gVisor,

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.550
which really helps it scale.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.840
And here's an example
of a load test

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:41.560
where a serverless service
for Cloud Functions

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:43.510
is sent several
thousands of requests.

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:46.390
And we see that all of
the requests are served.

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:47.800
And none of them are dropped.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.490
And the Cloud functions scaled
seamlessly in this test.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.940
Along with availability
and scalability,

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:57.160
users really care about
openness and persuasiveness

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:01.310
of their application
and serverless service.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:03.490
And here's a key differentiator
for Cloud Functions

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.860
in this space where
Cloud Functions,

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:07.780
with the underlying
Functions framework that

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.090
runs it and the build
mechanism to build it,

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:12.340
have all been open-sourced.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:14.495
As a result, a user
can take their function

00:23:14.495 --> 00:23:16.120
and deploy it to
Cloud Functions, which

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:17.680
is a fully managed product.

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:20.220
They can run it locally
in their favorite IDE

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:21.970
while they're on a plane.

00:23:21.970 --> 00:23:25.060
They can also take that function
and run it in any environment

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.712
where you can run a container.

00:23:26.712 --> 00:23:29.170
This could be Cloud Run, which
is our serverless containers

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:32.200
product, or any other
container environment

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.590
in other clouds as well.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:37.290
This is a really key
differentiator for the product.

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:40.220
And now I'd like to
invite Dustin on stage

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:42.550
to provide you a
demo on functions

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:45.282
everywhere to walk you
through this vision.

00:23:45.282 --> 00:23:46.990
DUSTIN INGRAM: If
we're a developer who's

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:48.920
not used to working
with containers,

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:50.410
let's see how we
can use buildpacks

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.480
to take our existing
Cloud Function,

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:54.670
with its simple
developer experience,

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:57.310
and have it automatically
turn into a container

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:00.190
that we can run anywhere,
including the powerful Cloud

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:03.370
Run, all without having
to understand Dockerfiles

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:05.980
or how to create a container.

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:08.380
You can use buildpacks
to turn your source code

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:11.350
into a containerized
application, either locally,

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:14.830
using the pack command, or
in Google Cloud Build, which

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:16.490
has support in alpha.

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:19.630
Buildpacks will look at the
source code of your application

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:22.480
and figure out what is the
language, what is the runtime,

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:24.580
and what will it take
to turn your application

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:26.295
into a container?

00:24:26.295 --> 00:24:27.670
So in this case,
I'm going to use

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:29.380
the local pack build command.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.360
And we'll give it a
destination where we

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.260
want to publish the container.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:35.780
We'll also let it know that
it's a Google Cloud function.

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:37.780
This is the name of the
function within our file

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:40.280
that we want to run.

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:41.830
So if we go to my
container registry

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:45.040
and refresh, you can see, here
is the container that we just

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.870
built, see, just now.

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:48.530
And again, containers
are portable.

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:51.520
So I can deploy to Kubernetes
Engine or virtual machines

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:52.690
on Compute Engine.

00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:54.190
But let's deploy
it on Cloud Run,

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:58.180
because it's fully managed,
just like Cloud Functions.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:24:59.830
Once you're using
Cloud Run, you have

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:02.030
some advanced
configuration settings.

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:04.210
So you can customize
how much memory it has.

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:06.170
You can give it two CPUs.

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:08.500
And you have a longer
time-out in Cloud Run.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:10.160
You can also do concurrency.

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.460
So one running instance
of your application

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.650
could handle multiple
requests, which

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:18.970
might lead to fewer cold starts
and more efficient usage.

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:21.640
And potentially, you can set
the minimum and maximum number

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:23.470
of instances.

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:25.240
Now that it's
deployed, you can see

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:29.650
that Cloud Run gives us a URL,
just like Cloud Functions.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:32.442
But you can also specify
your own domain name.

00:25:32.442 --> 00:25:34.150
Let's post some more
temperature readings

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:37.180
to that app that is
running on Cloud Run.

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:39.010
And if I pop over
here to Firestore,

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:40.930
you can see the data coming in.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:42.490
Click here, and we
can prove that it

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:44.650
is, in fact, going into
the Cloud function that

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:46.960
is running on Cloud Run.

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:49.450
So we were able to take our
existing Cloud function,

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:51.850
with just source code,
and, using buildpacks,

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:54.130
turn it into a
containerized application

00:25:54.130 --> 00:25:57.197
that can be run anywhere,
even the powerful Cloud Run.

00:25:57.197 --> 00:25:58.780
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN:
Thank you, Dustin,

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:01.930
for that amazing demo
on functions everywhere.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:06.732
Please check out Cloud Functions
at cloud.google.com/functions.

00:26:06.732 --> 00:26:08.190
Please also check
out our Functions

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.940
Framework and buildpacks,
which are open-sourced.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:13.570
The GitHub links
are provided here.

00:26:13.570 --> 00:26:15.700
In addition, if you
have any questions,

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:19.730
please feel free to reach
out to Dustin or myself.

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:21.790
That's a wrap of our session.

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:25.410
Thank you, Dustin and Matthew,
